# libepoxy -- resolves GL entry points at runtime so GTK does not have to know
# at compile time which extensions the driver will turn out to support.
#
# -Dglx=yes -Dx11=true, AND THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE OTHER TWO CORRECTIONS
# IN THE SAME CASCADE. The clause it replaces read:
#
#   "glx and x11 are off to match mesa, which is built without GLX. GTK reaches
#    GL through EGL here."
#
# BOTH SENTENCES ARE STILL TRUE. mesa is still built -Dglx=disabled, there is
# still no libGL.so.1 providing GLX on this system, and GTK still reaches GL
# through EGL. Unlike cairo's xlib surface -- where the old reason confused
# drawing to an X drawable with running an X server -- nothing here was
# miscategorised. THE GLX DISPATCH PATH ENABLED BELOW GENUINELY CANNOT WORK ON
# THIS TREE TODAY.
#
# It is enabled anyway, because gtk4 gives no choice: gdk/x11/gdkglcontext-x11.h
# line 33 includes <epoxy/glx.h> and nearly every X11 source includes that
# header; gdkglcontext-glx.c is in gdk_x11_public_sources UNCONDITIONALLY; and
# gtk4's meson.options has no glx option at all. There is no such thing as
# gtk4-with-X11-without-GLX. The consumer at the end of the chain is
# mutter-x11-frames, which mutter builds unconditionally under Xwayland.
#
# WHY SHIPPING A DISPATCH PATH THAT CANNOT WORK IS SAFE HERE, established by
# reading both trees rather than assuming:
#   gtk4 tries EGL FIRST (gdkdisplay-x11.c 2984-3006, and its own comment says
#   so), falls to GLX only if picky EGL fails, and if GLX also fails tries EGL
#   AGAIN with the pickiness off. GLX is a middle option, not the floor.
#   In the arm where GLX IS reached, gtk4 calls epoxy_has_glx() first
#   (gdkglcontext-glx.c 978). That reaches epoxy_load_glx(FALSE, true)
#   (dispatch_glx.c 161), which passes exit_if_fails=FALSE into
#   get_dlopen_handle (dispatch_common.c 556). The failed dlopen is swallowed at
#   the (void)dlerror() branch on line 313 -- NOT the abort() on line 316 --
#   epoxy_has_glx returns false, and gtk4 reports "GLX is not supported" and
#   moves on. Had that call passed true, this would have been a crash on
#   fallback and the flag would not be set.
#
# -Dx11=true is not separable: libepoxy's meson errors outright with "GLX
# support is explicitly enabled, but X11 was disabled" (meson.build 51-57), so
# the two flags are one decision. libX11 is already packaged and already earlier
# in the build order; no new package.
#   AND IT IS A BUILD DEPENDENCY ONLY, which the first draft of this recipe got
#   wrong by declaring it at runtime. The built libepoxy.so.0 has exactly two
#   NEEDED entries -- libc and the loader -- and zero undefined X11 symbols:
#   this library resolves GL and GLX alike by dlopen, so it links neither. X11
#   is needed to compile, because the installed epoxy/glx.h includes X11/Xlib.h,
#   and epoxy.pc says "Requires.private: x11, egl" so consumers of that header
#   need it too. Caught by readelf on the built artefact rather than by
#   reasoning about what a GLX library ought to link.
#
# WHEN THIS BECOMES LIVE, and this comment and finding 45 must be read together
# because neither is complete alone: the GLX dispatch path here starts working
# the day the recorded restore route runs -- libglvnd, then mesa built with GLX,
# then the xwayland flag. Finding 45 holds that route and the human's deferral
# of it; this comment holds the code that is waiting for it. If either is
# changed, the other is now wrong, which is exactly the neighbour-note rot this
# tree keeps finding -- so it is written as a mutual reference at creation time
# rather than discovered later.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/anholt/libepoxy/archive/1.5.10/libepoxy-1.5.10.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=a7ced37f4102b745ac86d6a70a9da399cc139ff168ba6b8002b4d8d43c900c15
SRC_DIR=libepoxy-1.5.10

# A GitHub archive URL ends in the bare tag.
SRC_FILE=libepoxy-1.5.10.tar.gz

MESON_ARGS="-Dglx=yes -Dx11=true -Degl=yes -Dtests=false"
